To generate a table of contents automatically in LaTeX, you simply need to include the `\tableofcontents` command in your document where you want the table to appear.

Here is a complete, minimal working example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

% Optional: Change the title of the Table of Contents
\renewcommand{\contentsname}{Table of Contents}

\begin{document}

% 1. Insert the Table of Contents
\tableofcontents 
\newpage % Moves the actual content to the next page

% 2. Create sections to populate the Table of Contents
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section. LaTeX will automatically number this as "1".

\subsection{Background}
This is a subsection. It will appear indented in the ToC as "1.1".

\section{Methodology}
This section explains the methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Details about data collection.

\subsubsection{Surveys}
A subsubsection (typically not numbered by default in the 'article' class, 
but included in the ToC depending on depth settings).

\section{Results}
The results of the experiment are presented here.

\section{Conclusion}
The final section of the document.

\end{document}
```

### Key Commands Explained

1.  **`\tableofcontents`**: This is the primary command. It tells LaTeX to generate the table here. It automatically collects all headers defined by `\section`, `\subsection`, etc., and lists them with their corresponding page numbers.
2.  **Sectioning Commands**: Commands like `\section{Title}`, `\subsection{Title}`, and `\chapter{Title}` (in book/report classes) are the input sources. Every time you use one of these, LaTeX adds an entry to the `.toc` file.

### Important Note: Compilation Process
In LaTeX, the table of contents is generated from an auxiliary file (`.toc`). Therefore, **you must compile your document twice** to see the correct result:

1.  **First Run:** LaTeX creates a `.toc` file containing the list of sections and page numbers.
2.  **Second Run:** LaTeX reads the `.toc` file and actually prints the table in the document.

If you only compile once, the table of contents may appear empty or outdated.

